  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 337 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA deep upper trough parked over southern Canada near the \nPacific Northwest will move eastward through Tuesday into \nWednesday and flatten out the upper ridge that is over much of \nthe Plains. During this timeframe near the surface, a cold front\nwill push across the northern Plains throughout late Monday \nthrough Tuesday. This front will come into contact with a moist \nairmass provided by strong southwest flow ahead of it. Combine \nthis with modest but sufficient wind shear, increased chances \nfor isolated showers and thunderstorms will linger into Tuesday \nmorning. Once the front passes through, slightly cooler \ntemperatures will be in its wake for the remainder of the week, \nwith highs in the upper 80s to to potentially low 90s further \nsouth along I80. Wednesday in particular will be the coolest \nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s across the region. \n\nThe flattened ridge will become a more zonal flow regime through the \nmid week with high pressure settling across Nebraska. This is likely \nto preclude precipitation chances until the following weekend where \na deepening upper trough is forecasted to take shape across the \nPacific Northwest sometime Saturday morning. Once this trough \ncrosses the Rockies, it will support increasing precipitation \nchances as well as the potential fore returning to near normal highs \nfor the time of year in the low to mid 80s.",
